# Code Review, reduced to what M1's parser accepts.
# See `bug.json` beside this file for the shape all seven hold to, for why a
# reduced form exists at all, and for the three ways the designed samples under
# `crates/core-model/domain/workflow-samples/` are stale against the parser --
# `question` where it reads `criteria[]`, `declare_plan_at` inside
# `evidence_scope` where the registry parents it to the step, and
# `max_context_size`, which M1 does not read. `reference_docs` was a fourth and
# is not any more -- M1 reads it. The questions below are ported from the
# designed files; none of the shape is.
#
# The designed `code-review.json` is already linear and already three steps --
# `read`, `assess`, `deliver` -- so nothing here is folded. What is still
# dropped: the `manifest_rule:review_gate` on `deliver`, which is refused as
# `OutsideM1`. The `artifact_exists` checks were dropped with it and are back --
# that mechanical-check type is in M1's carried set now, and the last paragraph
# below says what it is doing here. `assess`'s `evidence_type` is `facts_note`
# rather than the designed `review_findings`, which is not a value the schema
# has -- see `[review-findings-evidence-type]` in `docs/OPEN.md`.
#
# **The Judge panel on `assess` is what this workflow most needs and is the one
# it cannot have yet.** This is the one workflow whose gaming patterns exist for
# it and nothing else: `no_findings_on_substantial_diff`,
# `findings_not_tied_to_changed_lines` and `findings_generic` describe a review
# that went through the motions. A review workflow with no semantic tier is the
# exact failure those patterns name -- it produces prose, and prose is not a
# claim. The paragraph below says what stands in the way.
#
# `read` and `deliver` carry no Judge. Reading is not a work product, and
# delivering is a hand-off.
#
# **`assess` still carries no Judge, and #153 is not what is holding it.** A
# written work product reaches the Judge now, so the note `assess` produces
# would. What does not reach it is the diff the review is about: on Code Review
# the diff is the Drone's *input* rather than its output, so it is not in the
# worktree and `WorkProduct::patch` reads nothing. Every criterion and every
# `flag_if` pattern this step would declare -- does the review engage with what
# the diff does, are the findings tied to changed lines, would they read the
# same about a different diff -- is a question about that diff.
#
# `no_findings_on_substantial_diff` and its two siblings go through
# `GamingBrief`, which takes a patch and nothing else. On this workflow that
# patch is empty, which is the same shape of blindness #153 named and a
# different cause.
#
# This is the "Code Review inverts" exception `docs/concepts/workflow.md` flags
# against its own evidence-type table. It needs a step whose work product is a
# review of a diff Armada holds somewhere it can read.#
# **A step whose product a later step reads writes a file.** `artifact_exists`
# is carried now, and `bug.json` carries the reasoning: a written step used to
# hand the next one three prose strings, two of which `verification::submission`
# says nothing routes on. Fleet opens the path this step declares and puts the
# file's contents in the Judge's brief as the document the step produced; the
# three strings are shown beside it, labelled as the summary. The mechanical
# check reads only the file's size, so an empty file stops the step and whether
# what is in it is any good stays the Judge's question.
#
# **The `artifact_exists` checks the designed file declares are restored.** The
# paragraph above that said "every `artifact_exists` check" was dropped because
# the type is not in M1's carried set is no longer true -- it is. The designed
# targets are a registry name on `read` and `REVIEW.md` on `assess`; M1 has no
# artifact registry, so both are paths. On this workflow they matter more than
# anywhere else: `assess` has no Judge and cannot have one until the diff it
# reviews is somewhere Armada can read, so a `read` step that produced nothing
# would otherwise reach `assess` and `deliver` unnoticed.
#
# **`model: haiku` on `deliver`, and nowhere else.** `bug.json` carries the
# reasoning and the cache cost. `read` and `assess` are the workflow -- one
# writes the note the other is checked against, the other is the judgement this
# workflow exists to produce, and `assess` has no Judge over it, so the model
# reading the diff is the only thing standing between a real review and prose.
# Both name none. `deliver` is the hand-off, which is what the paragraph above
# already calls it.
{
  "version": 1,
  "workflow_id": "code_review",
  "name": "code_review",
  "structure": "linear",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "read",
      "label": "Read the diff",
      "evidence_type": "facts_note",
      "mechanical_checks": [
        {
          "type": "artifact_exists",
          "target": ".armada/artifacts/read.md"
        }
      ],
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "assess",
      "label": "Assess",
      "evidence_type": "facts_note",
      "mechanical_checks": [
        {
          "type": "artifact_exists",
          "target": ".armada/artifacts/assess.md"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_scope": {
        "context_source": "hybrid",
        "exclude_paths": [
          ".env",
          "node_modules/",
          "target/"
        ]
      },
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "deliver",
      "label": "Deliver the review",
      "evidence_type": "bundle",
      "model": "haiku",
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    }
  ]
}
